so we're here today for content creation  but before you start making lots of  content for your newly online course I  want you to take a step back and think  do I really need to be creating content  right now am I ready if your course is  set up and you really feel you need your  own content your own video that's great  and I will tell you a little bit about  that today but there are amazing  resources and I've worked with two teams  this week putting together lists and  resources for you I worked with we  create edu a collective of content  creators on YouTube and I'm gonna put a  link in the video description to the  spreadsheet we made and this features  diverse fields from history and  linguistics to Natural Science you can  find a lot of content for your courses  there also this week I worked with the  American Society for biochemistry and  molecular biology and we made an  incredible overview of how to get  started with online teaching and  learning and we put a bunch of resources  so if you're in those fields  there's many links and lots of things  that you can use already but you've  decided to go for it you want to create  your own content think about the  simplest way you could do it you know  you're used to maybe walking in front of  a lecture room and maybe writing on the  board maybe showing some slides perhaps  you can just make a screen recording of  you talking through those slides so path  of least resistance here let's try to  make our content in a way that we're  comfortable with that will be easy for  us the first time through if you find  you're about to download a new software  and get really deep maybe that's not  what you want to be doing this week in  video 3 of this series I talked about  doing synchronous sessions with your  students and you can use an office hours  or a problem-solving session record it  and use little chunks of it as your  course material you'll be interacting  with the students so you'll get  questions and that's really great  content to deliver and if you're going  to do a synchronous office hours anyway  why not use it as your course content in  video 3 we also talked a little bit  about live streaming and I showed you  how to do it with maybe a whiteboard  behind you since the easiest way to do a  live stream is just taking a recording  of you and your surroundings but if you  have this PowerPoint lecture that you  were just dying to present you can do  your live stream provide your students  with the slides say now go to slide  three say what you wanted to say and  pause for questions the students can  type their questions into the live  stream you can answer them in real-time  and you can present this wonderful  interactive version of your PowerPoint  lecture the live stream remember it goes  directly into YouTube which has an  editor you can chop up your video into  smaller segments you can just use pieces  of it that you want you can put it  online as is without any processing  whatsoever so that's a very convenient  way to get content online quickly okay  for audio my colleague Mary puffins Roth  says that the air pods Pro are a really  great way if you want to just do office  hours I have done office hours on just  the little white earbuds that come with  the Mac plugged into my computer and the  audio is just fine it's not the greatest  thing you've ever heard but recording in  the room is kind of an off sound and  people do not tolerate bad audio as well  as they'll tolerate bad visuals so you  want to get the audio sounding at least  tolerable so people want to listen to it  so I don't recommend or talking directly  into your computer use a headset or if  you want to go a little fancier I'm  wearing my rode love mic right now  so comes in this little pouch and I have  it hooked up to my phone since I'm  filming on my camera but you can plug  this directly into your computer and set  up a screen recording software to record  you through your love mic this is about  70 bucks but my preferred is this blue  snowball here and there's other  different brands but this is the one  that I use it's a podcast microphone and  this is just a pop filter in front of it  that prevents my C's from sounding too  too hard and things like that it is  absolutely beautiful sound and  many of the animated biochemistry videos  that I do about proteins if you want to  sample some of that audio I'm using that  mic for this and I use this mic  throughout this series okay so for your  quick videos if you're recording  something do the lighting as best you  can you don't have to have a fancy setup  like this I record off a doc cam I used  to just put two lights like this on  either side shining at my piece of paper  to illuminate it a little bit and my  early videos they're not perfect but  they're absolutely fine the students  could see what I was drawing so you  don't need a fancy lighting set but do  make sure there's no weird shadows or  anything before you get started  always just do a quick test run of your  recording to make sure things look just  about the way you want right now I'm  just using the lights in the room and I  think you can see me just fine so you  don't have to get crazy or fancy with  this most of my videos feature  hand-drawn structures because I do  organic chemistry and so I like to draw  everything out it's the way I'm  comfortable it's the way I'm very quick  at doing this I don't need to generate  big powerpoints with animations I can  just hand draw and use my screen  recording software so I'm going to show  you a little demonstration of what I'm  using to do my hand-drawn stuff and I'll  show you a quick hack where you can plug  your iPad directly into your computer  and do a quick drawing that way I've  gotten a lot of questions about my  document camera this is the most  expensive part of my setup this is a  Luminex ladybug DC 125 and it lists at  about 250 I believe I got it on sale  seven years ago for 160 and it has just  been the cornerstone of my online  education I like to draw it makes things  easy  I can produce videos very quickly and  that's a real benefit to this another  benefit is that it comes with its own  software so what you're seeing on the  screen is just a live video of what I'm  doing here so I can be drawing this I  could be gesturing to this I could even  put a piece of paper over this and do a  slow reveal of something that I already  drew so this allows me to really create  content  very quickly it projects like this and  then I record my screen with my screen  recording software so you see a theme  here if I go into my screen recording  software I can just set it up to be  recording the the desktop and recording  from my blue snowball here and when I  press this button I'll jump over to this  view and then I can talk about it into  my mic and later I can just chop out  things if I don't think the students  need to see me drawing all these  structures I'll just chop it out and  talk about the structures and so that's  what you can see in some of my organic  chemistry videos now if you're not in  the market today for a 250 dollar  document camera you can get cheaper ones  however I also have a hack for you that  just involves using your iPad so I've  done this in a pinch before using Adobe  sketch I can record this screen so I'll  set up my recording to record so I don't  need to do the desktop now but I can  record from this iOS device so if you  have an external device you can hook  into this there are also things like  writing tablets I don't use those so I'm  not really gonna get into it but I just  want you to know that they exist so I  won't record video because them not  sitting in front of the computer right  now I'll record my audio from my blue  snowball and I can go ahead and set this  up it'll count down to record and then I  can go ahead and talk while I draw on my  iPad and whenever I'm done I can finish  my recording so now you can see the  frame from my iPad loaded in maybe I  don't want all these brushes on here  there are key commands to crop your  screen so you can just take out anything  you don't want to see and I'll go ahead  and just play it and so you can see me  drawing if I wanted to cut the video I  can just press T and it will trim my  Clips so I can cut out pieces very  readily and so that's just a wonderful  way to get something down really quickly  if you want to draw you don't want to  buy a duck cam and you have an iPad  handy well thanks for joining me today  this is my last video of this series and  I made it through a video a day for a  week so I'm really excited right now  the take-home message from this video is  just if you want to create content do it  as simply as possible and remember that  in the video description I'm gonna put a  few links so that you can see all the  other great content out there and decide  if this is an area you really want to  delve into right now thanks again for  joining me
 

